{"title":"Kimberling’s point X(22)","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span id=\"Exeter_point_X.2822.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Exeter_point_X(22)\">Exeter point X(22)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Kimberling%E2%80%99s_point_X(22)&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Exeter point X(22)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Exeter_X22.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/4/44/Exeter_X22.png/500px-Exeter_X22.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"500\" height=\"462\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/4/44/Exeter_X22.png/750px-Exeter_X22.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/4/44/Exeter_X22.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Exeter point is the perspector of the circummedial triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/c/07cae99696024474b84a05b4a84f91579e8e7131.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_0B_0C_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"61\" height=\"15\" /> and the tangential triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/b/a6b641c242b66290184ce4e40ad54861b8ddaa35.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A&#39;B&#39;C&#39;.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"63\" height=\"16\" /> By another words, let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> be the reference triangle (other than a right triangle). Let the medians through the vertices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/9/299ddf7c0f03ea46bf09f81f93adeea0756aa15c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B, C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"58\" height=\"16\" /> meet the circumcircle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/2/9e2b196e9b7e57d1ec99f6534c581ea9759d2170.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Omega$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/d/ded21d28ee089ef0edfa210c35d1d473aa646d1f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_0, B_0,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"57\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/e/7ce4664de7db214d4c56a42b1922cbd0fb7e7785.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> respectively. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/2/022948689ac03ea1705a6af78b86d32ae2a369eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A&#39;B&#39;C&#39;$\" width=\"55\" height=\"14\" /> be the triangle formed by the tangents at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/f/ccf6d387ce491c0ca100c134021f21f616d75162.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, B,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"40\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/8/de82b5de37f029271b42d807a7a35ac5a7e8e7b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Omega.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> (Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/9/1090530ae883b627e090bfb5e450ef796febca40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"16\" /> be the vertex opposite to the side formed by the tangent at the vertex A). Prove that the lines through <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/8/eb877bd52afc2e4cfd025de615082ad4dcfe9746.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_0A&#39;, B_0B&#39;,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"19\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/b/08b33e2e401ff3a3cb33dbe07332c71aca641ba8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_0C&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"41\" height=\"19\" /> are concurrent, the point of concurrence lies on Euler line of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/f/95fdfbb0362c49460ab4b7ef61acc59d3b0fac79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"49\" height=\"18\" /> the point of concurrence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/8/1e8713b50c2334d6f9fda77b03cf9cd725dc8a4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$X_{22}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"31\" height=\"17\" /> lies on Euler line of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/c/1ac8e715f5d59e18960d6d5162183a3be4729fa6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC, \\vec {X_{22}} = \\vec O + \\frac {2}{J^2–3} (\\vec H – \\vec O), J = \\frac {|OH|}{R},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"332\" height=\"40\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> - circumcenter, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> - orthocenter, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/f/eff43e84f8a3bcf7b6965f0a3248bc4d3a9d0cd4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> - circumradius.\n</p><p><i><b>Proof</b></i>\n</p><p>At first we prove that lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/b/8/eb877bd52afc2e4cfd025de615082ad4dcfe9746.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_0A&#39;, B_0B&#39;,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"19\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/b/08b33e2e401ff3a3cb33dbe07332c71aca641ba8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_0C&#39;$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"41\" height=\"19\" /> are concurrent. This follows from the fact that lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/e/a8e18602e5460875af10e59b16828e13dedb28d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AA_0, BB_0,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"86\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/1/371d03d750211ee0510a8a5c14dcbeb04c97477c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CC_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"33\" height=\"15\" /> are concurrent at point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <i><b>Mapping theorem</b></i>.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/b/20b5016493b0d7c21d955a96116263208b10c8a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_1, B_1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"54\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/b/70b97eb4ad6e4e7a5a97e2547d9ed6e965960d0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"18\" height=\"15\" /> be the midpoints of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/8/2782ccf4ca9cf31227fb7efa5433ce62f2739f36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC, AC,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"67\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/4/d3431b737f3671c3c1d717f039984ff101e97404.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"32\" height=\"16\" /> respectively. The points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/a/a0ab206aa1bc945cc1a0f0200f489fb637cb68ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A, G, A_1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"72\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/c/7cce761941ab4b57ae903d05b8fa0568ebbdd98f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"19\" height=\"15\" /> are collinear. Similarly the points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/8/3389bbaf294e76d319c239d354e8742f9ce9dffa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B, G, B_1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"73\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/1/71145755882ef0f1e12d10935523847f1692373e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"20\" height=\"15\" /> are collinear.\n</p><p>Denote <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/e/22ec107d9afef9af9ebb250e7321724484a3f482.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I_{\\Omega}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"17\" /> the inversion with respect <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/8/de82b5de37f029271b42d807a7a35ac5a7e8e7b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Omega.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />  It is evident that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/5/b1565f416fe1b18bbfaef5e259bdefec55989f28.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I_{\\Omega}(A_0) = A_0, I_{\\Omega}(A&#39;) = A_1, I_{\\Omega}(B_0) = B_0, I_{\\Omega}(B&#39;) = B_1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"422\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Denote <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/b/d7b6558c3cb13a0c3de7bd526ec216e2b77a69ed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_A = I_{\\Omega}(A&#39;A_0), \\omega_B =  I_{\\Omega}(B&#39;B_0) \\implies$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"293\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/8/32838bc902b85a5cc78d31ea85b347ccec11ff63.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A_0 \\in \\omega_A, A_1 \\in \\omega_A, O \\in \\omega_A, B_0 \\in \\omega_B, B_1 \\in \\omega_B, O \\in \\omega_B \\implies O = \\omega_A \\cap \\omega_B.\\]\" width=\"570\" height=\"16\" />\n</p><p>The power of point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> with respect <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/d/ced81803985be92fafdebaecd2c6bb99e77b20af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"21\" height=\"10\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/d/38db10ed206790c0264d909162b73cc011562c87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$GA_1 \\cdot GA_0 = \\frac {1}{2} AG \\cdot GA_0.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"205\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>Similarly the power of point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> with respect <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/a/f8a61b8667b8ee0dbb1231aa0845a526438b73ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"21\" height=\"10\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/7/7572f02bff5c1ef768b55fbc3a768e157a6f3bd3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$GB_1 \\cdot GB_0 = \\frac {1}{2} BG \\cdot GB_0.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"206\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/c/a7c2eb0f20563f61e5b08523ce8af138a6137c19.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G = BB_0 \\cap AA_0 \\implies AG \\cdot GA_0 = BG \\cdot GB_0 \\implies G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"423\" height=\"17\" /> lies on radical axis of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/d/ced81803985be92fafdebaecd2c6bb99e77b20af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"21\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/e/a/8ea1f9d7bc7eb95deb1be5fad90d21029bfa0ad9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_B.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"30\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>Therefore second crosspoint of  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/d/ced81803985be92fafdebaecd2c6bb99e77b20af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"21\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/a/f8a61b8667b8ee0dbb1231aa0845a526438b73ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega_B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"21\" height=\"10\" /> point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> lies on line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/0/2a001d743af320a4435c551b34c5313c164adce4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OG$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" /> which is the Euler line of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> as desired.\n</p><p>Last we will find the length of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/b/43b7840fe7d4408ae1b40e9e77b4443ad0c4951e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$OX_{22}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"17\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/c/c5ceb04b834cbc2bbe6340ad8f5974c17b8d49b7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[A_1 = BC \\cap AA_0 \\implies AA_1 \\cdot A_1A_0 = BA_1 \\cdot CA_1 = \\frac {BC^2}{4}.\\]\" width=\"445\" height=\"39\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/6/066900197794168e15ac24cf6d950d578268a775.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[GO \\cdot GD =GO \\cdot (GO+ OD) =  GA_1 \\cdot GA_0\\]\" width=\"339\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/b/72b18bee6c7f8dff0edf702ddd0cbbca6858d014.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[GA_1 \\cdot GA_0 = \\frac {AA_1}{3} \\cdot ( \\frac {AA_1}{3} + A_1A_0) =  \\frac {AA_1^2}{9} +   \\frac {BC^2}{3 \\cdot 4} = \\frac {AB^2 + BC^2 +AC^2}{18}= \\frac {R^2 – GO^2} {2}.\\]\" width=\"649\" height=\"39\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/0/6b042d19196e6709e5a8b5eefb8440fa9da8b4f8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2GO^2 + 2 GO \\cdot OD =  R^2 – GO^2 \\implies  2 GO \\cdot OD =  R^2 – 3GO^2.\\]\" width=\"473\" height=\"17\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/3/3/b33b3f7bc4f3b919c0ad6a9d4d81e8b5b0d24f21.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[I_{\\Omega}(D) = X_{22} \\implies  OX_{22} = \\frac {R^2} {OD}    = \\frac {R^2 \\cdot 2 GO}{R^2 – 3 GO^2} = \\frac {2 HO}{3 – \\frac {HO^2}{R^2}} = \\frac {2}{3 – J^2} HO\\]\" width=\"532\" height=\"47\" /> as desired.\n</p><p><i><b>Mapping theorem</b></i>\n</p>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Transformation.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/e/e2/Transformation.png/500px-Transformation.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"500\" height=\"246\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/e/e2/Transformation.png/750px-Transformation.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/e/e2/Transformation.png/1000px-Transformation.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Let triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> and incircle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/d/54d7d48553f4d9e7ab418118607ea324cbfddfda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> be given.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/e/e2e94693a56b5f5ff57ba598d083f5fe92f59ded.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[D = BC \\cap \\omega, E = AC \\cap \\omega, F  = AB \\cap \\omega.\\]\" width=\"318\" height=\"16\" />\nLet <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> be the point in the plane <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/2/bd222166def59a3315433877917a826de6488031.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"49\" height=\"14\" />\nLet lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/e/0/4e0f2009d86fc99c36dcbe0ab71216b0905b2f6f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DP, EP,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"69\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/a/24ad2dad196a24708c4c056a01a28329e79688d5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$FP$\" width=\"28\" height=\"12\" /> crossing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/d/54d7d48553f4d9e7ab418118607ea324cbfddfda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> second time at points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/a/c7a091d3317d261c8f7971d38e7232726156e980.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D_0, E_0,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"58\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/d/dcd4ea84e5e5b5b8157383b4b12d844d7ab48a12.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F_0,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"23\" height=\"16\" /> respectively.\n</p><p>Prove that lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/6/976c166c36cd52ff31b436f5103085ac7e340808.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD_0, BE_0,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"87\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a050ac7c141ef25cf40259ad0e11fa0fc356d225.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CF_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"17\" /> are concurrent.\n</p><p><i><b>Proof</b></i>\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/d/14dbd6ca0c930f623b2904c5e1011da5d7ec411c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[k_A = \\frac {\\sin {D_0AE&#39;}}{\\sin {D_0AF&#39;}} = \\frac {D_0E&#39;}{D_0A} \\cdot \\frac {D_0A}{D_0F&#39;} =  \\frac {D_0E&#39;}{D_0F&#39;}.\\]\" width=\"330\" height=\"40\" />\nWe use Claim and get: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/0/450958d22c9be25c35d1e35cceed74493231accf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_A =  \\frac {D_0E^2}{D_0F^2}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"100\" height=\"44\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/6/ba66d5bb48cfaceca1e60aa83991c9621a6795d7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[k_D = \\frac {\\sin {D_0DE}}{\\sin {D_0DF}} = \\frac {D_0E}{2R} \\cdot \\frac {2R}{D_0F} =  \\frac {D_0E}{D_0F} \\implies k_A = k_D^2.\\]\" width=\"430\" height=\"40\" />\nSimilarly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/9/1794c329683744fb1bb3cd3e62a4c6b2f31223c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k_B = k_E^2, k_C = k_F^2.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"146\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p>We use the trigonometric form of Ceva's Theorem for point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/2/872e3b09425c9659d782119a543dc858aa520a40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle DEF$\" width=\"60\" height=\"13\" /> and get \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/2/912314ceabb1baeeeeb9df01d098800ab939f1f2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[k_D \\cdot k_E \\cdot k_F = 1 \\implies k_A \\cdot k_B \\cdot k_C = 1^2 = 1.\\]\" width=\"337\" height=\"19\" />\nWe use the trigonometric form of Ceva's Theorem for triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> and finish proof that lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/6/976c166c36cd52ff31b436f5103085ac7e340808.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AD_0, BE_0,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"87\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a050ac7c141ef25cf40259ad0e11fa0fc356d225.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CF_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"17\" /> are concurrent.\n</p><p><br />\n<i><b>Claim (Point on incircle)</b></i>\n</p>\n<figure class=\"mw-halign-right\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Point_on_incircle.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/4/4f/Point_on_incircle.png/450px-Point_on_incircle.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"450\" height=\"221\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/4/4f/Point_on_incircle.png/675px-Point_on_incircle.png 1.5x, https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/thumb/4/4f/Point_on_incircle.png/900px-Point_on_incircle.png 2x\" /></a><figcaption></figcaption></figure>\n<p>Let triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> and incircle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/d/54d7d48553f4d9e7ab418118607ea324cbfddfda.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\omega$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> be given.  \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/f/10fabc0f6adf1653aaeaa2e90b70bda09f6f7f30.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[D = BC \\cap \\omega, E = AC \\cap \\omega, F  = AB \\cap \\omega, P \\in \\omega, F&#39; \\in AB,\\]\" width=\"452\" height=\"17\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/d/47d871d13e2d442e7ceae01ed919495392cf3221.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[PF&#39; \\perp AB, E&#39; \\in AC, PE&#39; \\perp AC, A&#39; \\in EF, PA&#39; \\perp EF.\\]\" width=\"424\" height=\"17\" />\nProve that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/6/186dae24cd0bed91f513ecfd36d44a8be3bb10ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac {PF&#39;}{PE&#39;} = \\frac {PF^2}{PE^2}, PA&#39;^2 = PF&#39; \\cdot PE&#39;.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"259\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p><i><b>Proof</b></i>\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/2/7921c86e05c6682033f66ba4754b29073544917a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[AF = AE \\implies \\angle AFE = \\angle AEF = \\angle A&#39;EE&#39;.\\]\" width=\"356\" height=\"14\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/c/f6ca16247c0782c83ecffbb3b6724365faf850fa.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\angle EFP = \\angle PEE&#39; \\implies \\angle PFF&#39; = \\angle PEE&#39; \\implies\\]\" width=\"373\" height=\"14\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/b/82b7af65e564dd36998f842e05b581d318ec3ab6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\triangle PFF&#39; \\sim \\triangle PEA&#39; \\implies \\frac {PF}{PF&#39;} = \\frac {PE}{PA&#39;}.\\]\" width=\"300\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>Similarly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/d/a1df70d512ea5ed13e946961f66e56f84dc5a956.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle PEE&#39; \\sim \\triangle PFA&#39; \\implies \\frac {PE}{PE&#39;} = \\frac {PF}{PA&#39;}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"305\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p>We multiply and divide these equations and get:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/1/131061f99d4a05f88a138dda53cf5674f9be22b6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[PA&#39;^2 = PF&#39; \\cdot PE&#39;, \\frac {PF&#39;}{PE&#39;} = \\frac {PF^2}{PE^2}.\\]\" width=\"255\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p><b>vladimir.shelomovskii@gmail.com, vvsss</b>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018092630\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.037 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.054 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 275/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 0/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 2/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 17768/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    0.000      1 -total\n-->\n\